.TH "mbedded\-cbor\-structs" "7" "2026-11-08" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH Databind
\f[CR]cbor::structs<T>\f[R] writes a C++ value as CBOR and reads it
back.
An aggregate struct needs no code of its own: g++ 16 with
\f[CR]\-std=c++26 \-freflection\f[R] reads its members by reflection,
and every other compiler reads them through Boost.PFR 1.84 or later
(C++23 and the Boost headers; the library ships a copy for a system that
has none, see the README).
A class with a constructor or a base class takes a description (section
3).
basics.md states how errors come back and how long a result stays valid.
The reference with examples that run in the browser:
https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/structs.html.
.SS 1. Write a struct and read it back
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] address {
    std::string city;
    std::string zip;
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] person {
    std::string name;
    int age;
    std::vector<std::string> tags;
    std::optional<address> home;
};

\f[B]auto\f[R] bytes = cbor::structs<person>::encode(person{\(dqann\(dq, 30, {\(dqadmin\(dq}, address{\(dqGraz\(dq, \(dq8010\(dq}});
\f[B]if\f[R] (!bytes)
    \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(bytes.error());
cbor::diagnostic_notation(*bytes);
\f[I]// {\(dqage\(dq: 30, \(dqhome\(dq: {\(dqzip\(dq: \(dq8010\(dq, \(dqcity\(dq: \(dqGraz\(dq}, \(dqname\(dq: \(dqann\(dq, \(dqtags\(dq: [\(dqadmin\(dq]}\f[R]

\f[B]auto\f[R] const back = cbor::structs<person>::decode(std::move(*bytes));
\f[B]if\f[R] (!back)
    \f[B]return\f[R] std::unexpected(back.error());
person const &p = **back;
p.home\->city;   \f[I]// \(dqGraz\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]encode\f[R] returns a
\f[CR]std::expected<std::string, cbor::error>\f[R] with the bytes.
\f[CR]decode\f[R] returns a
\f[CR]std::expected<cbor::ref<person>, cbor::error>\f[R]:
\f[CR]*back\f[R] is the \f[CR]ref\f[R], and \f[CR]**back\f[R] is the
\f[CR]person\f[R].
A struct is a map with the names of its members as keys.
\f[CR]cbor::diagnostic_notation\f[R] prints the bytes as text.
